-- Refresh the bundled sample without overwriting customised learner content.

update public.activities set content = '{"objectives":["Distinguish an asset, a threat event, a weakness and a control.","Write a risk statement that links evidence to a business consequence."],"markdown":"## Think of a café\n\nCybersecurity is like running a café with a stockroom, a till and an order board. The stockroom needs a lock so only authorised staff can enter. The order board must be accurate so the kitchen prepares the right meals. The till has to work during the lunch rush. Each protection serves a different business need.\n\n## Work through one clear example\n\nA repair shop keeps customer contact details in a shared spreadsheet. The **asset** is the customer data. The **weakness** is that everyone can share the file externally. The **threat event** is someone sending the link to the wrong recipient. The **consequence** is that a stranger could read customer contact details. A **control** is to disable public links and give sharing permissions only to people who need them.\n\nWrite the risk this way: *Because the file allows public links, an accidental share could expose customer contact information to someone outside the business.*\n\n| What you know | What you would check |\n| --- | --- |\n| The example says anyone can share the file. | Review the actual sharing configuration and a sample of permissions. |\n| The file contains customer details. | Confirm what fields it stores and whether each field is needed. |\n| Disclosure is possible. | Check activity logs before claiming it actually happened. |\n\n## True incident: Medibank, Australia, 2022\n\nThe Australian privacy regulator’s court filing describes a cyberattack in which personal and health information was accessed and subsequently published online. The filing puts the number of people exposed to risk at approximately **9.7 million**. Sensitive details can be used for extortion and identity fraud, and their disclosure can cause distress even when a person suffers no financial loss.\n\n**What reduces the risk?** Require strong multi-factor authentication for privileged and remote access, remove unused access, minimise sensitive data retained, and monitor for unusual access or large transfers. Verify these controls with evidence. For example, check how MFA is enforced for an administrator and whether an alert is reviewed. These are defensive lessons from the scenario, not a claim that one control would have prevented the incident.\n\n[Source: Australian Information Commissioner’s concise statement](https://www.oaic.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0025/221974/Australian-Information-Commissioner-v-Medibank-Private-Limited-concise-statement.pdf)","takeaways":["Start with the asset and the people or service that could be affected.","Separate a potential consequence from an outcome supported by evidence.","Choose a control you can verify."]}'::jsonb
where id = 'intro' and chapter_id = 'cia-triad' and content = '{"objectives":["Distinguish assets, threats and controls"],"markdown":"A customer contact list is an **asset**. Accidental disclosure is a **threat event**. Restricting access to staff who need the list is a **control**. Record what could happen, what it would affect, and which evidence supports your judgment.","takeaways":["Describe the asset and consequence before choosing a control."]}'::jsonb;

update public.activities set content = '{"objectives":["Classify consequences using confidentiality, integrity and availability.","Choose a control and identify evidence that it works."],"markdown":"## Picture a shared booking diary\n\nA clinic keeps patient bookings in a diary. **Confidentiality** means only authorised people can read it. **Integrity** means dates and details are accurate. **Availability** means staff can retrieve it when they need to manage appointments. A locked cupboard can help with confidentiality, but it would hurt availability if nobody on duty could open it.\n\n| Objective | Everyday example | What can happen | How to reduce the risk |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Confidentiality | A customer list is shared with an external recipient. | Private details could be misused or shared further. | Limit access and review sharing links regularly. |\n| Integrity | An accidental edit changes a booking from Tuesday to Thursday. | The customer misses the appointment and staff waste time resolving it. | Limit edit rights and keep version history. |\n| Availability | The booking calendar is unavailable on a busy day. | Staff must reschedule visits or make decisions without timely information. | Test recovery from backup and practise a fallback process. |\n\n## True incident: WannaCry and the NHS, England, 2017\n\nIn May 2017, WannaCry disrupted NHS organisations in England. The National Audit Office reported that thousands of appointments and operations were cancelled. NHS England estimated that more than **19,000 appointments** were cancelled overall, based on the data it had. In five areas, patients had to travel farther for emergency care. The primary consequence in this example is **availability**. The fact that a service was disrupted does not establish that patient data was stolen.\n\n**What reduces the risk?** Keep a device inventory, set deadlines for critical updates and verify patch coverage. Use network separation to limit disruption across services, test backup recovery and maintain a rehearsed fallback for critical work. A backup job marked successful is useful evidence, but a timed restore test tells you whether the service can actually recover.\n\n[Source: UK National Audit Office investigation](https://www.nao.org.uk/reports/investigation-wannacry-cyber-attack-and-the-nhs/)","takeaways":["Classify a finding by its consequence. One event can affect more than one objective.","Link recommendations to the consequence they reduce.","Ask for evidence that a control works in practice."]}'::jsonb
where id = 'cia' and chapter_id = 'cia-triad' and content = '{"objectives":["Classify consequences using the CIA triad"],"markdown":"**Confidentiality** limits disclosure. **Integrity** protects accuracy and completeness. **Availability** keeps information and services usable when needed. If a fictional bakery cannot retrieve its order list during service, the primary impact is availability.","takeaways":["A single event can affect multiple CIA goals."]}'::jsonb;

update public.activities set content = '{"objective":"Record two evidence-based risks in a fictional bakery.","prerequisites":["Read the CIA triad lesson"],"tools":["Text editor or spreadsheet"],"setup":"Create a risk register with these columns: Evidence, asset, what could happen, CIA impact, recommended control, verification.","safety":"Use only the fictional information supplied in the lab.","steps":[{"id":"assets","title":"List assets","instructions":"The bakery records customer names, contact numbers, order details and due dates in one shared spreadsheet. Identify the key information asset and explain why it matters.","expectedResult":"A named asset and a sentence about the business process it supports.","hint":"Ask what the bakery would struggle to make or deliver if the spreadsheet were missing or wrong.","workedAnswer":"**Asset:** Customer order and delivery information. Staff use it to prepare the right items by the due date and contact customers if an order changes."},{"id":"risks","title":"Record risks and controls","instructions":"Anyone on staff can edit every order. The owner says backups run, but has never tested a restore. Record one risk for each observation. Suggest a control and describe how you would verify it.","expectedResult":"Two entries with evidence, business consequence, CIA impact, a control and a verification step.","hint":"A wrong due date is an accuracy problem. An order list you cannot recover is an access problem. Start each statement with the evidence you were given.","workedAnswer":"| Evidence | What could happen | CIA impact | Control | Verify it |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| All staff can change all orders. | An accidental edit could change a due date and cause a missed delivery. | Integrity | Limit edits to staff who maintain orders and enable version history. | Review editing permissions and recover a test cell from version history. |\n| No restore has been tested. | A lost file could leave staff without an order list during trading. | Availability | Schedule a restore test and record how long recovery takes. | Restore a fictional copy and verify that order details and dates are intact. |"}],"troubleshooting":"If a consequence is unclear, name the task the bakery would be unable to do. If an observation is unverified, record it as a question to investigate."}'::jsonb
where id = 'risk-walkthrough' and chapter_id = 'cia-triad' and content = '{"objective":"Record two evidence-based risks","prerequisites":["Read the CIA triad lesson"],"tools":["Text editor"],"setup":"Create a blank risk register.","safety":"Use only the supplied fictional information.","steps":[{"id":"assets","title":"List assets","instructions":"The bakery stores orders in one shared spreadsheet. Identify the information the bakery relies on.","expectedResult":"Order details recorded as an asset."},{"id":"risks","title":"Record risk and control","instructions":"Anyone on staff can edit orders, and no backup has been tested. Write one risk for each observation and suggest a control.","expectedResult":"Two entries with evidence, impact and a proposed control."}],"troubleshooting":"If a consequence is unclear, describe what the bakery would be unable to do."}'::jsonb;
